# Status of This Proposal #

This document is an early, implementation-oriented proposal from the
`browser-to-mcp` project. It is not a W3C Recommendation, an MCP
specification, or an endorsement by the Web Machine Learning Community Group
or the Model Context Protocol project.

The proposal is published to make the design reviewable before implementation.
Names, fields, wire behavior, and security requirements may change based on
experiments and community feedback.

The current implementation target is a local, read-only MCP server. The
proposal intentionally separates the portable workflow contract from a
particular browser provider, LLM, or hosting service.

# 1. Introduction #

Many useful business capabilities exist only behind a web user interface.
Generic browser-control tools can operate those sites, but an agent must
rediscover the same page structure and policy on every run. That creates
unnecessary model calls and makes behavior difficult to review.

This proposal defines a small contract for turning a reviewed browser workflow
into a narrow MCP tool:

    workflow contract -> validate -> policy check -> replay -> typed result

The resulting tool is a business capability such as `search_hacker_news`, not
a collection of unrestricted `click` and `type` primitives.

## 1.1 Goals ##

The proposal aims to provide:

* human-reviewable and versionable workflow definitions;
* deterministic browser replay for supported steps;
* typed MCP inputs and structured outputs;
* explicit domain, action, and side-effect policy;
* assertions that determine whether a run succeeded;
* redacted traces and provenance for every invocation;
* a clear fallback relationship with APIs and WebMCP.

## 1.2 Non-goals ##

The proposal does not define:

* a browser automation engine;
* a universal recorder or autonomous workflow learner;
* a replacement for Playwright, Browser Use, or native APIs;
* a public registry of credentials or workflows;
* permission to bypass CAPTCHA, access controls, or site policy;
* autonomous execution of payments, messages, destructive actions, or uploads.

# 2. Terminology #

An **author** creates or reviews a workflow contract.

A **workflow** is a versioned sequence of declared browser operations,
assertions, policies, inputs, and outputs.

A **runner** executes a validated workflow in a browser context.

A **compiled tool** is the MCP tool metadata and handler derived from a
workflow. The compiled tool MUST NOT contain undeclared capabilities.

A **run** is one invocation of a compiled tool.

**Page content** includes visible text, DOM values, tool descriptions, and
tool outputs received from a page. Page content is untrusted data.

# 3. Workflow Contract #

The canonical authoring format is YAML. Implementations MUST parse it into a
typed internal model before serving or executing it. An implementation MAY
provide another authoring format if it produces the same canonical model.

The following example is illustrative and deliberately read-only:

<pre class="lang-yaml">
name: hn_search
version: 1
description: Search Hacker News and return visible matching results.
risk: read_only

permissions:
  allowed_domains:
    - hn.algolia.com
  downloads: false
  uploads: false
  submit_forms: false
  send_messages: false
  spend_money: false

inputs:
  query:
    type: string
    required: true
    min_length: 1

steps:
  - id: search
    action: goto
    url: https://hn.algolia.com/?q={{query}}
    timeout_seconds: 30
  - id: results
    action: extract_text
    selector: main

outputs:
  results:
    type: string

success:
  - id: results_present
    assertion: output_not_empty
    field: results
</pre>

## 3.1 Required invariants ##

An implementation MUST reject a workflow that:

1. has no stable name and version;
2. has an undeclared or unsupported action;
3. navigates outside its allowed domains;
4. has an input that is not represented in its typed schema;
5. has no success assertion for its declared output;
6. requests a side effect disallowed by its permissions;
7. changes its canonical manifest without review;
8. attempts to use page content to change workflow policy or tool metadata.

The validator SHOULD report the source path and a stable error category.

## 3.2 Canonical manifest ##

The runner MUST compute a canonical representation of the workflow and a
manifest digest before execution. The digest SHOULD cover the workflow name,
version, description, permissions, input schema, steps, output schema, and
success assertions. Tool metadata SHOULD be derived from this same canonical
representation so that metadata changes are diffable and reviewable.

# 4. Execution Model #

The normative execution order is:

1. load and parse the workflow;
2. validate the typed contract;
3. validate caller input;
4. check the manifest and runtime policy;
5. create an isolated browser context;
6. enforce domain and action permissions before each step;
7. execute the declared steps;
8. evaluate success assertions;
9. assemble typed output and provenance;
10. write a redacted trace before returning the result.

An implementation MUST fail closed when validation, policy, assertion, or
manifest checks fail. It MUST NOT silently switch to an unconstrained agent
loop. A recovery system MAY propose a workflow patch, but a patch MUST be
reviewed and revalidated before it is used.

## 4.1 Runtime priority ##

When a site offers multiple integration surfaces, an implementation SHOULD
prefer them in this order:

1. an explicitly configured first-party API;
2. native WebMCP tools exposed by the page;
3. deterministic browser replay;
4. a bounded semantic recovery adapter;
5. human review or a structured failure.

WebMCP discovery and execution are separate capabilities. A runtime MUST NOT
claim that a discovered WebMCP tool is safe to execute merely because it is
discoverable.

# 5. MCP Mapping #

Each valid workflow maps to one narrow MCP tool:

* `name` comes from the workflow name;
* `description` comes from the reviewed workflow description;
* `inputSchema` comes from the typed input definitions;
* the result contains the declared output and provenance;
* failures use stable structured categories rather than opaque browser errors.

An implementation SHOULD load workflows dynamically at server startup and
MUST refuse to expose invalid workflows. It SHOULD support local STDIO first;
remote HTTP transport, OAuth, and long-running task handles are later
deployment concerns and MUST NOT weaken the workflow policy.

Example result shape:

<pre class="lang-json">
{
  "status": "success",
  "output": { "results": "..." },
  "provenance": {
    "workflow": "hn_search",
    "version": 1,
    "manifestDigest": "sha256:...",
    "runId": "run_...",
    "sourceUrls": ["https://hn.algolia.com/"],
    "completedAt": "2026-07-22T00:00:00Z"
  }
}
</pre>

# 6. Security And Privacy #

The workflow contract is a security boundary, not only a prompt for an agent.

Implementations MUST:

* use explicit origin/domain allowlists;
* isolate browser contexts unless a caller explicitly selects a labelled
  profile;
* redact credentials, cookies, tokens, and configured sensitive fields from
  traces and outputs;
* treat page text, tool descriptions, and extracted values as untrusted;
* prevent page content from changing permissions, schemas, or step targets;
* require explicit review before enabling write, financial, upload, message,
  or destructive actions;
* record refusals and policy decisions for auditability.

Implementations SHOULD expose `readOnlyHint`-style metadata where supported,
but metadata is advisory. Enforcement MUST happen in the runner.

The model must not be treated as an authorization system. Authorization is
derived from the validated workflow, caller identity, browser profile policy,
and runtime configuration.

# 7. Reliability And Interoperability #

Browser workflows are sensitive to layout changes, timing, authentication
state, localization, and anti-automation controls. A conforming implementation
SHOULD provide:

* stable selectors and explicit waits;
* bounded timeouts and retries;
* assertions for every important output;
* failure categories for selector, assertion, authentication, timeout,
  permission, schema, browser, and recovery failures;
* reproducible fixtures for changed layouts, delayed pages, malformed output,
  unauthorized navigation, and hostile page text;
* run metrics including success rate, latency, retry count, and failure class.

The workflow contract SHOULD remain portable across browser providers. A
provider-specific capability MUST be declared rather than inferred.

# 8. Conformance Levels #

**Level 0: Contract.** Parses typed workflows, validates policy, computes a
manifest digest, and rejects invalid contracts.

**Level 1: Replay.** Implements declared read-only steps, assertions, typed
outputs, redacted traces, and provenance.

**Level 2: MCP.** Exposes validated workflows as MCP tools and returns the
structured result shape.

**Level 3: Interoperability.** Discovers and safely adapts to native WebMCP,
APIs, existing browser sessions, and bounded recovery without bypassing the
contract.

An implementation MUST state its conformance level and unsupported actions.

# 9. Open Questions #

The following are intentionally not frozen:

* whether a common workflow schema should be proposed to MCP or remain
  project-local;
* a standard selector and locator portability model;
* remote workflow distribution and signing;
* OAuth and profile isolation for remote execution;
* a common trace and evaluation format;
* how native WebMCP execution should be authorized and represented;
* whether long-running runs should use MCP Tasks or a project-neutral handle.

# 10. Implementation Roadmap #

The first implementation should ship Level 0 through Level 2 for local,
read-only workflows. The reference workflow is Hacker News search. After
repeatability and trace quality are measured, the project can add authenticated
read-only workflows, existing-tab attachment, downloads, native WebMCP
execution, repair proposals, remote transport, and hosted operations.

The proposal will be revised from implementation evidence, conformance tests,
security review, and feedback from MCP and Web platform practitioners.

# Appendix A: Relationship To Existing Projects #

Playwright MCP provides generic browser-control tools for an agent. Browser Use
and similar systems provide semantic browser-agent execution. WebMCP defines a
web-page-side API for sites to expose JavaScript or declarative tools to
browser-integrated agents.

This proposal occupies a different layer: it defines how a developer authors,
validates, replays, governs, and exposes a reviewed browser workflow as an
external MCP tool when a first-party API or native WebMCP tool is unavailable.

